===Noun===
{{en-noun|parts of speech}}

# {{senseid|en|Q82042}}{{lb|en|grammar}} A [[linguistic]] category of words sharing [[syntactic]] or [[morphological]] behaviour and [[semantic]] properties, such as [[noun]] or [[verb]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1828|author=Charles Follen|title=A Practical Grammar of the German Language|page=9|location=Boston
|passage='''PARTS OF SPEECH'''. There are ten '''parts of speech''', viz. Article, Substantive or Noun, Adjective, Numeral, Pronoun, Verb, Adverb, Preposition, Conjunction, and Interjection.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1844|author=E. A. Andrews|title=First Lessions in Latin; or Introduction to Andrews and Stoddard's Latin Grammar|edition=6th|page=91|location=Boston
|passage=322. The '''parts of speech''' which are neither declined nor conjugated, are called by the general name of particles. 323. They are adverbs, prepositions, conjunctions, and interjections.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=One group of substantives, the gerunds (''scolding''), belongs to a form-class with infinitives and with other verb-forms, in serving as head for certain types of modifiers, such as a goal (''scolding the boys''). For this reason a system of '''parts of speech''' in a language like English cannot be set up in any fully satisfactory way: our list of '''parts of speech''' will depend upon which functions we take to be the most important.|title=Language|author=Leonard Bloomfield|year=1933|page=269}}
#* '''2008 (1894)''', B. L. Gildersleeve & G. Lodge, ''Gildersleeve's Latin Grammar'', third edition, reprint by Dover, p.9:
#*: The '''Parts of Speech''' are the Noun (Substantive and Adjective), the Pronoun, the Verb, and the Particles (Adverb, Preposition, and Conjunction)[.]
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2018|author=James Lambert|title=Setting the Record Straight: An In-depth Examination of ''Hobson-Jobson''|journal=International Journal of Lexicography|volume=31|issue=4|doi=https://doi.org/10.1093/ijl/ecy010|page=489|text=Following this, coding was added to delineate multiple senses within entries and nested terms within entries; then '''part of speech''' labels were added for every sense[.]}}

